  "answer_text": "Yes, parathyroid disease **can** cause or be associated with neck pain, though it manifests in several different ways depending on the type and severity of the condition. Here's a detailed breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd34 Direct Neck Symptoms\n\nA common symptom of parathyroid disease is a **lump in the neck**, as parathyroid tumors can grow anywhere along the thyroid gland. In later stages, tumors can grow large enough to be felt during daily activities like swallowing or talking.\n\nIn cases of **parathyroid carcinoma** (cancer), neck pain can be a presenting symptom. A 45-year-old woman who presented with non-specific **neck and shoulder pain** was found to have mild hypercalcemia, markedly elevated parathyroid hormone levels, and an irregular parathyroid gland on imaging.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddb4 Bone Pain (Including Neck/Spine)\n\nThe most common symptoms of hyperparathyroidism include **chronic fatigue, body aches, difficulty sleeping, bone pain**, memory loss, poor concentration, depression, and headaches.\n\nOsteoporosis \u2014 a common complication of parathyroid disease \u2014 can lead to broken bones and fractures. If one of these fractures happens in the **spine or neck**, which are particularly fragile areas, it could even cause significant pain or more serious complications.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Serious/Advanced Disease\n\nIn more severe cases, parathyroid disease \u2014 particularly parathyroid carcinoma \u2014 should be considered in the presence of **multiple pathological fractures, muscle atrophy, generalized bone pain, hypercalcemia, and a clear neck mass**.\n\nA condition called **parathyromatosis** \u2014 characterized by the seeding and growth of hyperfunctioning parathyroid tissue throughout the neck or mediastinum \u2014 can further complicate the clinical picture and lead to recurrent and refractory hypercalcemia.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\ude7a Other General Symptoms of Parathyroid Disease\n\nParathyroid disease also frequently leads to **osteoporosis, kidney stones, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias, and kidney failure**.\n\nHyperparathyroidism is a serious disease that becomes very destructive with time, and over time it can lead to problems throughout the body, including **osteoporosis, high blood pressure, kidney stones, kidney failure, stroke, and cardiac arrhythmias**.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Bottom Line\n\nWhile **neck pain is not always the most common or primary symptom** of parathyroid disease, it can occur \u2014 especially due to tumor growth in the neck region, bone loss/fractures in the spine, or in cases of parathyroid carcinoma. If you are experiencing persistent neck pain along with symptoms like fatigue, bone aches, kidney stones, or excessive thirst/urination, it is worth speaking with a doctor to check your calcium and parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels.",
